.TH FOXTERM 1 "2026" "FOX 2.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
foxterm \- FOX terminal emulator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B foxterm
[\fB--profile\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB--term\fR \fIvt100\fR|\fIxterm\fR]
[\fB--working-directory\fR \fIdir\fR]
[\fB--opacity\fR \fIpercent\fR]
[\fB--execute\fR \fIcommand\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B foxterm
opens a native window with a terminal view. It starts the default shell on a
pseudo terminal and accepts text, keys, mouse reporting, selection and clipboard
operations. A window holds one tab per session in a tab strip; a tab can be
detached into its own window without interrupting the running child.

Closing a tab whose child is still running asks for confirmation when the
profile sets \fBconfirm\fR. A child that exits closes its tab when the profile
sets \fBcloseonexit\fR; otherwise the tab keeps the final screen and its title
gains the suffix \fB[exited]\fR. Closing the last tab of the last window ends
the application, and closing a window that still has running tabs asks once for
all of them. A termination signal quits without a confirmation dialog.
.PP
A right click in the terminal view opens a context menu with Copy, Paste,
Select All, Find, Clear Scrollback, Reset Terminal, the character encoding, New
Tab, Detach Tab and Close Tab. An entry that cannot act is disabled, not
hidden. While the child has mouse reporting on, the right button
belongs to the child and Shift+right click still opens the menu. A right click
on a tab opens Duplicate, Rename, Detach, Close and Close Others for that tab;
the entries are disabled when the press is on the empty part of the strip.
\fBReset Terminal\fR clears the scrollback as the terminal reset sequence does.
.SH CHARACTER ENCODING
The Character Encoding submenu selects the encoding of the active session. The
default is UTF-8. The set is UTF-8, ISO-8859-1, ISO-8859-2, ISO-8859-15,
Windows-1250, Windows-1251, Windows-1252, KOI8-R, CP437 and CP850; the checked
entry is the encoding the tab uses. A switch takes effect at once: further
output is decoded with the new set, keys, pasted text and IME text are encoded
with it, and the screen is neither cleared nor re-decoded, so text that is
already on the screen keeps its characters. The child is not restarted and is
not told about the change.
.PP
A single byte encoding decodes each byte above 0x7F to one character and draws
it in one cell; a byte the set leaves empty becomes the replacement character.
In a single byte encoding there are no eight bit control sequences, so a byte
like 0x9B is a character and not the start of a control sequence. Keys and
pastes are converted to the session encoding; a character the encoding cannot
express is sent as B?R and the status line reports it.
.PP
Every profile stores the encoding a new tab starts with in
B[PROFILE.IidB] encodingR. The profile dialog edits it. The encoding
belongs to the view, not to the child: the locale the child inherits, and so
the charset it writes, is the one the session was started with.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --profile \fIname\fR
Select a profile. The built-in profile is \fBdefault\fR.
.TP
.B --term \fIvt100\fR|\fIxterm\fR
Select the terminal emulation and TERM value.
.TP
.B --working-directory \fIdir\fR
Start the child in \fIdir\fR.
.TP
.B --execute \fIcommand\fR [\fIarg\fR ...]
Run \fIcommand\fR directly, without a shell.
.TP
.B --opacity \fIpercent\fR
Set the whole-window opacity, 0 to 100. The backend must support it; see
\fBisOpacitySupported\fR in the FOX manual. The value is stored in the
application registry.
.TP
.B --help
Show usage information.
.TP
.B --version
Show the FOX version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
The child inherits the environment and receives
.BR TERM ,
.BR COLUMNS ,
.BR LINES ,
.BR TERM_PROGRAM ,
and, for xterm mode,
.BR COLORTERM .
The default shell is taken from
.B COMSPEC
on Windows and
.B SHELL
on POSIX systems.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR fox-config (1)
